WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.560
One generation after heavy-labelled E. coli switched to ordinary nitrogen, all of

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.340
its DNA formed one band.

3
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.000
That band sat halfway between heavy and light.

4
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.800
Why did none remain fully heavy?

5
00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:22.163
To follow old DNA, Meselson and Stahl grew cells with heavy nitrogen.

6
00:00:23.543 --> 00:00:27.603
Old strands appear purple; strands built after the switch appear cyan.

7
00:00:28.983 --> 00:00:33.563
In a caesium chloride gradient, heavy DNA settles lower than light DNA.

8
00:00:35.283 --> 00:00:36.863
Hybrid DNA settles between them.

9
00:00:37.867 --> 00:00:40.927
Three models start from the same purple duplex.

10
00:00:42.227 --> 00:00:46.787
Conservative copying keeps both old strands together and builds a separate cyan

11
00:00:46.787 --> 00:00:53.247
duplex. Semiconservative copying separates the old strands, then gives each a new

12
00:00:53.247 --> 00:01:00.047
partner. Uniform dispersive copying breaks the old material into segments and mixes

13
00:01:00.047 --> 00:01:01.727
those segments through both daughters.

14
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:05.140
A band is not a picture of a helix.

15
00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:10.000
It marks many DNA molecules with the same buoyant density.

16
00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:14.960
One intermediate band could mean every molecule is half old.

17
00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:21.680
It cannot reveal whether that half is one intact strand or many segments until

18
00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.880
another generation separates their predictions.

19
00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:27.733
Watch each model make its initial daughters.

20
00:01:28.913 --> 00:01:33.913
Which models can put every molecule at the hybrid position, with no heavy or light

21
00:01:33.913 --> 00:01:34.913
band?

22
00:01:40.217 --> 00:01:44.857
Conservative copying makes one heavy duplex and one light duplex.

23
00:01:45.997 --> 00:01:51.177
A single hybrid band after one generation rules out conservative copying.

24
00:01:52.657 --> 00:01:55.977
Semiconservative copying makes two purple-cyan hybrids.

25
00:01:57.817 --> 00:02:02.457
Uniform dispersive copying can also put equal old and new material in every

26
00:02:02.457 --> 00:02:05.917
molecule. Both survive the initial test.

27
00:02:06.917 --> 00:02:10.657
Now let the survivors copy again in ordinary nitrogen.

28
00:02:12.157 --> 00:02:16.037
Semiconservative copying keeps each purple strand in one hybrid.

29
00:02:17.337 --> 00:02:18.957
The other daughters are fully cyan.

30
00:02:20.357 --> 00:02:22.897
Its tube gains separate hybrid and light bands.

31
00:02:24.297 --> 00:02:29.277
Uniform dispersive copying halves the old fraction inside every molecule again.

32
00:02:30.617 --> 00:02:32.377
Its single band shifts toward light.

33
00:02:32.377 --> 00:02:33.377
Semiconservative copying

34
00:02:33.417 --> 00:02:39.377
After two generations, the extracted DNA formed hybrid and light bands in equal

35
00:02:39.377 --> 00:02:45.497
amounts. That matches the semiconservative distribution, not one uniformly mixed

36
00:02:45.497 --> 00:02:52.077
band. Hybrid and light bands after two generations rule out uniform dispersive

37
00:02:52.077 --> 00:02:55.857
copying. The old material never disappears.

38
00:02:57.137 --> 00:03:01.017
It only spreads among more molecules, and where it sits is decisive.

39
00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:05.980
The experiment tracked inherited material, not enzymes.

40
00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:12.900
Heating the hybrid DNA separated heavy and light subunits, consistent with old and

41
00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:17.820
new strands. Meselson and Stahl still stated their limit carefully.

42
00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.260
Their bands did not alone show that each conserved subunit was one polynucleotide

43
00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:25.260
chain.

44
00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:30.110
Each daughter molecule keeps one old strand and builds one new strand.

45
00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:35.490
That logic predicts later band patterns and gives replication its name:

46
00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:41.410
semiconservative. Keep the rule for chromosome replication in this lesson.
